FINDINGS:
1. [BLOCKER] src/runtime.rs:2038 — `validate_stack_tree` pushes both children onto its worklist with no visited-node set, so a forged self-consistent tree that reuses one child under both branches is walked exponentially. `collections.namedtuple` clones a `Value::Record` by cloning its `Arc`, so a Redblue program can build a valid-looking tree of depth ~53 with only 53 physical records; `collections.pop_left` then validates it through `deque_parts` at src/runtime.rs:1929 and visits ~2^53 logical nodes before hanging. — Track `Arc` identities or a hard node budget in `validate_stack_tree` and in `stack_pop_order`, and reject repeated nodes or over-budget trees before traversal; add a test with a valid shared-child DAG. — VERIFIED: traced `collections.namedtuple` at src/runtime.rs:2664 cloning shared `Value` storage, `validate_stack_tree` at src/runtime.rs:2038-2039 pushing both children without deduplication, and `deque_parts` calling it before every pop at src/runtime.rs:1929-1930.

2. [MAJOR] src/runtime.rs:1929 — every deque push/pop validates every node in both ends, making each legitimate operation O(n) and n operations O(n²); this undercuts the work-queue advantage claimed by `tests/collections_test.rs:525`, since `skip` is also O(n) per removal. The existing resource test only checks tree-node sharing, so it cannot detect the cost. — Validate on construction/ingestion with an internal integrity marker or budget, or at minimum test operation complexity and document the deque as O(n) per operation. — VERIFIED: `deque_parts` loops over every tree and calls `stack_tree_parts` -> `validate_stack_tree`; every push/pop path reaches it at src/runtime.rs:2570, 2581, 2592 and 2616.

